  (RUNTIME-EXCEPTION.md).
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  tmxlite.pcpp - the tmxlite unit: `import tmxlite;` reads Tiled maps
  (https://www.mapeditor.org: .tmx maps, .tsx tilesets, .tx object templates)
  from PaskalCPP. A self-describing vendor module: it owns every include path,
  library path and link library it needs, per official target. The library is
  libtmxlite.a (one per target), built by PaskalCPP-Libs' build-tmxlite.cmd
  from tmxlite's own CMake project, linked by @linklibrary: every module of
  the program shares the one copy, and a program links only the libraries it
  imports.

  The API is tmxlite's C++ API as it ships (https://github.com/fallahn/tmxlite),
  used straight from PaskalCPP: a `tmx::Map` variable, LMap.load(path) (true
  when the map and every external tileset and template it names were read;
  tilesets and images are found relative to the map's folder), then the
  getters: getOrientation, getTileCount (tiles across / down), getTileSize,
  getTilesets (each a tmx::Tileset: getFirstGID, getImagePath, getTile(gid)),
  getLayers (each a `tmx::Layer::Ptr`; getType() says which layer it is and
  getLayerAs<tmx::TileLayer>() / <tmx::ObjectGroup>() / <tmx::ImageLayer>() /
  <tmx::LayerGroup>() gives it as that), getProperties (tmx::Property:
  getName, getType, getIntValue, getStringValue, ...). A tile layer's
  getTiles() holds one tmx::TileLayer::Tile per cell, row by row: ID is the
  global tile id (0: empty), flipFlags the Horizontal / Vertical / Diagonal
  bits; an infinite map keeps its tiles in getChunks() instead. Tile layer
  data is read in every one of Tiled's "Tile Layer Format" choices: CSV,
  Base64 (uncompressed), and Base64 compressed with zlib, gzip or zstd
  (tmxlite is built with USE_EXTLIBS over the zlib, pugixml and zstd units,
  which this unit imports).
  LMap.loadFromString(xml, folder) reads a map already in memory. A failed
  load returns false and leaves the map empty.

  Files are read with the C++ library's streams and the C runtime, so a
  program that imports HookIO reads maps from an IO or a zip archive as well
  (tmxlite_ext's LoadTiledMapIO / LoadTiledMapZip do that, and draw the map
  with raylib).
==============================================================================*/

module unit tmxlite;

@addincludepath "$P:res/libs/vendor/tmxlite/include";

// The headers select dllimport on Windows unless TMXLITE_STATIC is set; the
// library was built with it, and every includer needs it too
@cppdefine "TMXLITE_STATIC";

// libtmxlite.a was built with USE_EXTLIBS: its layer decoders and XML reader
// are the zlib, pugixml and zstd libraries, each linked through its own unit
// (imported below; tmxlite's own headers include none of them)
@ifdef TARGET_X86_64_WINDOWS_GNU
  @addlibrarypath "$P:res/libs/vendor/tmxlite/lib/x86_64-windows-gnu";
@elseif TARGET_X86_64_LINUX_GNU
  @addlibrarypath "$P:res/libs/vendor/tmxlite/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu";
@else
  @message error "tmxlite: unsupported target";
@endif

@linklibrary "tmxlite";

import
  zlib,
  pugixml,
  zstd;

// The headers go to this unit's header, so every importer sees tmxlite's API
cpp header;

#include <tmxlite/Map.hpp>
#include <tmxlite/Layer.hpp>
#include <tmxlite/TileLayer.hpp>
#include <tmxlite/ObjectGroup.hpp>
#include <tmxlite/ImageLayer.hpp>
#include <tmxlite/LayerGroup.hpp>
#include <tmxlite/Tileset.hpp>
#include <tmxlite/Property.hpp>
#include <tmxlite/Object.hpp>

end.
